  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q25-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "25",
    "prompt": "Find the units digit of the sum\n$$1^1+2^2+3^3+\\cdots+100^{100}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "0.\n\nThe units digits repeat every 20 terms; each period sums to 94, so five periods sum to 470.",
      "worked": "Work modulo 10. For a fixed units digit, the positive powers repeat with a period dividing 4. Since 20 is divisible by 4,\n$$(k+20)^{k+20}\\equiv k^k\\pmod{10}\\qquad(k\\ge1).$$\nThe units digits for $k=1,\\ldots,10$ are\n$$1,4,7,6,5,6,3,6,9,0,$$\nand for $k=11,\\ldots,20$ they are\n$$1,6,3,6,5,6,7,4,9,0.$$\nEach list sums to 47. There are five complete periods, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sum_{k=1}^{100}k^k&\\equiv5(47+47)\\pmod{10}\\\\\\sum_{k=1}^{100}k^k&\\equiv470\\pmod{10}\\\\\\sum_{k=1}^{100}k^k&\\equiv0\\pmod{10}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe units digit is 0.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
  },
